-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): Native code replaces a definition only with the relation to its meaning
-- (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §9 “Native execution as a mathematical program transformation”). Native code today comes from an x86 translator whose output is bound and
-- run with no relation to the definition (src/lang.c, lib/x86.cub), from pasted machine code
-- (lib/elf.cub), and with proofs about its bytes at one fixed placement (lib/compile/); nothing
-- relates a fleet's proved behaviour to its binaries (lib/deploy.cub). Decided by Avik
-- (2026-11-14): native realization goes through C. "are you going to complete writing proven-
-- correct x86 RISC ARM impls instead of writing a C impl ? the value delivered to me by a complete
-- x86 machine code impl is near-zero. cant even run on a macbook can it ... there remains no
-- argument for x86 as far as i can tell." One C emitter runs wherever a C compiler does; per-ISA
-- emitters and their proofs would multiply by every target. The paper permits it (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…,
-- §9.3 “An exact decoded machine and a structural compiler theorem”, "Loading, native substitution, and export": "C and C++ text are optional target presentations. If an external compiler produces the executed bytes,
-- either its relevant compilation relation is assumed or those bytes receive their own checked
-- connection"). What follows: the emitter returns the relation between running the emitted C and
-- the term's meaning, which needs the meaning of the C fragment the emitter produces (a small,
-- fixed subset), not of all of C (that stays language support); the C compiler's relation is a
-- named assumption listed by #trusted; the evaluator substitutes native code only under that
-- relation. The x86 translator (src/lang.c, lib/x86.cub, lib/machine.cub, lib/elf.cub's pasted
-- machine code, lib/compile/) is superseded by it. The record that preceded the decision: plan
-- revision 9 (archive/2026-11-11/PLAN-rev9.md, revision 9, 9 Nov 2026, sha256 4d012c3a10fd…, §5 “The distinctions everything above x86 rests on”, item 5 “The target is x86-64”) had chosen x86 ("Not LLVM IR,
-- not C"); a session on 2026-11-12 planned C to break a cycle in its goal graph; neither was
-- Avik's.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/lang.c): wrong, against the paper (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…,
-- §9.3 “An exact decoded machine and a structural compiler theorem”, "Loading, native substitution, and export"): the
-- evaluator may replace a definition by native code only once a target-correctness witness relating
-- the code's execution to the definition exists. Here the bytes are bound after the only check
-- made, that the specialized term converts with the definition (lib/x86.cub jit-plan); the
-- translation from that term to bytes (x-push, x-lift, x-erase, the templates, this boundary's
-- marshaling) carries no relation, so a wrong translation would answer wrongly with nothing to
-- catch it.
